    {
      "id": "HMT-02",
      "title": "Decay of the integral in logarithmic cusp boxes",
      "chapter": "inputs",
      "anchor": "inputs-height-cusp",
      "statement": "On each of finitely many charts the height is at most C exp(2W). A unit logarithmic box has measure at most K exp(−2Φ(s)) throughout its allowed parameters. For A<N−1 the cone bound gives 2AW−2Φ≤C′−δ∑|sᵢ| with δ=2(N−1−A)/N>0. Summation over integer boxes is finite.",
      "hypotheses": "On the translated chart the triangular entries are bounded, the diagonal logs sum to zero and satisfy sᵢ₊₁≤sᵢ+T, and the remaining factor is unitary. The translating element is an L′-rational GL matrix up to place-dependent scalars, so a common denominator exists for its inverse and transpose. The packing needs at least one complex matrix factor and a Haar measure.",
      "definitions": [
        "W, sum of absolute differences of adjacent logs",
        "Φ, sum of signed differences over pairs in order",
        "unit logarithmic box and Haar packing",
        "geometric series over ℤᴺ"
      ],
